<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">New Kingdom of León</h1><p class="st-meta">1582 CE – 1821 CE · capital Monterrey</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Spanish frontier province of north-eastern New Spain, founded by the Portuguese-born Luis de Carvajal, from which modern Nuevo León and Monterrey grew.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1579 the Crown granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-carvajal-y-de-la-cueva">Luis de Carvajal y de la Cueva</a> a huge territory in the north-east, and he began settling it in 1582. After Carvajal's arrest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, his relatives having been accused of secretly practising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-montemayor">Diego de Montemayor</a> refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a> in 1596.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province was a thinly settled frontier of ranches and small mining camps. Settlers relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep and on the forced labour of local hunter-gatherers, captured under the congrega system, which provoked frequent resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a> remained a small town for most of the colonial era, governed by a governor under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1824, after independence, the province became the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-mexican-republic">First Mexican Republic</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a> later grew into one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico's</a> major industrial cities.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/new-kingdom-of-leon.jpg" alt="New Kingdom of León" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1582 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Monterrey</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Luis de Carvajal y de la Cueva, Diego de Montemayor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nuevo Reino de León</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1702&amp;focus=new-kingdom-of-leon" aria-label="New Kingdom of León on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2026 1233 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where New Kingdom of León lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2346 1489L2321 1717M2346 1489L2142 1327"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2142 1327h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2346 1489h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2346 1489h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Monterrey. <a href="/atlas?year=1702&amp;focus=new-kingdom-of-leon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

